very sexy .... essentially the elder bro of ramfs just that you have max size checking and that the underlying memory is using large pages so you ll 
likely have lesser dTLB misses. Believe me dTLB misses matter .. :-) For e.g., on your i7 x10 dTLB store misses reduction shaved off about a second from my write 
intensive app

Anyways to set it up heres how you do it:

<a> sysctl -w vm.nr_hugepages = num_pages
   after this you should read this value to know how many pages have been actually allocated

 OR
 
 you can set it up in your Linux boot-args by adding something like below. How to edit boot-args is distro dependent.
 " default_hugepagesz=1G hugepagesz=1G hugepages=56 ... "


<b> mount -t hugetlbfs -o uid=1001 -opagesize=1G[2M|...] size=  /mnt/path


<c> thats it now open a file under /mnt/hugetlbfs using O_CREAT | O_APPEND and mmap and thats it boom boom ..
    Btw please remember in your mmap use mapping size larger than page size else it will fail


Tested on 2.6.32-25-generic and used the "perf" app to measure reduction in dTLB accesses

